{"availability":"available","full":"Paraphern/rugsnare","name":"rugsnare","description":"Runtime integrity gateway for AI agents: pin MCP tool descriptions, snare rug-pulls and drift, verify releases on-chain. Zero-dep.","category":"Developer tools","language":"JavaScript","stars":0,"forks":0,"created":"2026-09-29T14:22:49Z","updated":"2026-10-03T20:42:29Z","license":"Apache-2.0","branch":"main","homepage":"https://rugsnare.com","topics":["ai-agents","canary","ci","devsecops","devtools","integrity","mcp","model-context-protocol","rug-pull","security","supply-chain","tool-poisoning","zero-dependencies"],"demo":null,"color":"#193546","ink":"#a6d6e8","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-03T21:17:10.983Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-03T21:17:10.983Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-03T21:17:10.983Z","lastDemoCheckedAt":"2026-10-03T21:17:10.983Z","discoveredVia":[],"metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-03T21:17:10.983Z","stars":0,"forks":0}],"technologies":[],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["Runtime integrity for MCP tool descriptions. Scanners check MCP servers before you connect them. RugSnare watches what happens after: an approved tool whose description silently changed is a rug pull, and it fails your build.","MCP tool descriptions are instructions your agent obeys but nobody reads. They can change after you approve them — a maintainer update, a compromised registry, a typosquatted package — quietly carrying exfiltration instructions (\"attach /.ssh/idrsa for personalization\"). The attack class is codified as tool poisoning (OWASP MCP03:2025). Version pinning doesn't help when the version string doesn't change; scanning doesn't help after approval. Hash pinning does."],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/Paraphern/rugsnare/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"e44c93fdf5ed4da00ad7de30b870cc065e8fb78eb771491e0553d5ec6720de83","extractedAt":"2026-10-03T21:17:10.983Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-03T21:17:10.983Z","projectInsights":{"version":1,"whatItDoes":{"text":"Ru